00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:48.020
Let me try to explain.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:52.810
All NASA spacesuits are pressurized, which makes them basically their own self-contained spacecraft.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:57.070
This pressurization is great when you're in a one-sixth gravity environment like the moon,

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.860
but you don't need to have all the suits pressurized for this testing.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.810
So NASA had some unpressurized suits made in Hollywood.

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:05.330
These suits are much lighter than the real suits,

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:07.950
but can still help test ergonomics and ease of movement.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.010
A couple of other benefits of the Hollywood suit

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:13.010
is that they'll be more comparable to the actual weight and feel of suits

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.990
once an astronaut gets in one-sixth gravity,

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:17.760
so astronauts will feel similar to the way it feels on the moon.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:21.080
And the suits won't have to be recharged like a pressurized suit does,

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:23.050
so work won't have to stop as often.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:26.120
Pretty cool, huh? Those suits are amazing.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.970
But obviously, real prototype spacesuits were tested as well.

